# Line endings, so a clone is the same everywhere.
#
# git's automatic conversion would give a Windows checkout CRLF and a
# Linux checkout LF. That is fine for most source, but not for this
# project:
#
#  * the shell scripts are run by bash. A CRLF shebang line makes bash
#    say "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" and the script does
#    nothing at all.
#  * the bridge core is published and its sha256 is in the release
#    feed, so the bytes have to be the same on every machine. git's
#    automatic conversion would make the working tree's copy differ
#    from the published one, and the feed's hash would name whichever
#    happened to be uploaded last.
#
# So: the shell scripts and the bridge are always LF, whatever the
# machine, and the rest of the text is LF in the repository and LF in
# the working tree too. The Windows batch files are the exception -
# cmd.exe wants CRLF - so they are always CRLF.

* text=auto eol=lf

# These must be LF everywhere: bash rejects a CRLF shebang line, and
# the bridge's bytes are named by a hash in the release feed.
*.sh text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf
bridge/ytbridge.py text eol=lf
website/ytbridge.py text eol=lf

# cmd.exe wants CRLF. It is the one file type that has to be the other
# way round.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
